\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
KCD2 uses a database of 300+ tables to hold most of the game data - items, buffs, perks, NPCs, etc... (see below for full list). These tables are loaded on startup and can never change during gameplay.\line 
For organizational purposes, tables can be split into\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506  multiple parts, which is then called\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
tableName__partName.xml}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 . These table parts are loaded together with the table (order in which they are loaded doesn't matter).\line 
As all game files, tables, and even table parts, can be overwritten by placing identically named file in the mod. However, since many mods need to modi\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
fy the same tables, this would create conflicts. Therefore we created a special process for patching a table - surgically modifying only a tiny part of the table.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Patching tables\~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Patching tables uses the same file format and naming scheme as table parts., except the table part name is identical to\~}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 
\ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 modid}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
. Such table part is then reffered to as "table patch". Table patches are loaded after all regular table parts are loaded, and are loaded in exact order - the loading order of mods, specified by mod_order.txt.\line 
There is one more difference - normal table p\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
arts must contain only new entires, while table patches can contain entries with same primary keys as the original table. When this happens, the entry is not added, but replaces (or changes) the original entry. How exactly this works depends on table type
.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 Old tables\~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid2980032\charrsid2980032 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 
Simple table format used in KCD1, still used for many tables that didn't change since then. It has a flat structure - each file has just a list of table rows. Relations between tables are handled by junction tables.\line 
If patching an old table, the table pat\hich\af31506\dbch\af31505\loch\f31506 ch must contain the entire row - all properties, even those that the mod doesn't change.\line }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
